{"content":"Found the bug: in OutputPythonFilter._process, the leading-newline check uses `if self.count >= 1:` but process() increments self.count before calling _process, so count is already 1 for the very first statement — causing an unwanted leading '\n' before `sql = '...'`. The PHP filter correctly uses `self.count > 1` for its analogous check. Fixing OutputPythonFilter to match that pattern.","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_1","name":"replace_text","arguments":{"path":"sqlparse/filters/output.py","old_text":"        # SQL query assignation to varname
        if self.count >= 1:
            yield sql.Token(T.Whitespace, '\n')","new_text":"        # SQL query assignation to varname
        if self.count > 1:
            yield sql.Token(T.Whitespace, '\n')"}}]}

